{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.85,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U1", "J12", "U2", "U3", "U4"],
      "pins": ["U1.19", "U1.27", "J12.A6", "J12.B6", "U2.5", "U3.5", "U4.5"],
      "nets": ["SCK"],
      "observed": "The single net named \"SCK\" carries the shared SPI clock of the three MAX31855s AND the USB D+ line: it contains U1 pin 19 (GPIO5/ADC2_CH0), U1 pin 27 (GPIO19/USB_D+), J12 pins A6/B6 (USB-C D+) and U2/U3/U4 pin 5 (SCK). The SPI clock is therefore shorted to the USB D+ data line and to the ESP32's native USB pin.",
      "expected": "The SPI clock net should connect only the ESP32 SPI clock pin and the three thermocouple SCK inputs. Per the spec the bus is on GPIO5 only, and the USB D+ line should connect only U1 GPIO19/USB_D+ to J12 D+. The two signals must be separate nets.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"32\") (name \"SCK\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"J12\") (pin \"A6\") (pinfunction \"D+\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"19\") (pinfunction \"GPIO5/ADC2_CH0\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"27\") (pinfunction \"GPIO19/USB_D+\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"SCK\") ..."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "SPI2 on GPIO5/GPIO10, CS pins GPIO4/6/7"}
      ],
      "impact": "The USB D+ line is bridged with the SPI clock (and with GPIO5/GPIO19). Every thermocouple read toggles SCK and corrupts the USB D+ line, and the USB-C connector plus the native USB pin load the SPI clock. USB programming/serial and reliable thermocouple communication cannot coexist; the board will not behave as the intended controller.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["J5", "U6", "U1"],
      "pins": ["J5.2", "U1.13", "U6.1", "U6.2"],
      "nets": ["/Microcontroller/HEATER"],
      "observed": "J5 is a 2-pin screw terminal whose pin 1 is on GND and whose pin 2 is on the \"/Microcontroller/HEATER\" net, i.e. the ESP32 GPIO1 / U6 IN+DIAG_EN logic control net. It is not connected to the switched heater output (U6 pin 7 OUT, which only reaches J6 pin 2 via Net-(J6-Pin_2)).",
      "expected": "A screw terminal on this board is a field-wiring connector (thermocouple terminals J1-J3, heater terminal J6, fan headers J7/J8). If J5 is a second heater/aux output it should be tied to the switched output (U6 OUT / J6 pin 2), not to a bare 3.3V MCU GPIO; the spec's heater connector is J6 and it never lists J5 on the HEATER net.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"4\") (name \"/Microcontroller/HEATER\") ... (node (ref \"J5\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"Pin_2\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"13\") (pinfunction \"GPIO1/ADC1_CH1/XTAL_32K_N\") ... (node (ref \"U6\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"IN\") ..."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "OUT (pin 7) feeds the heater connector J6 pin 2"}
      ],
      "impact": "Wiring a load to J5 would apply the load voltage directly to GPIO1 (destroying the MCU), and even shorting J5's two pins shorts GPIO1 to GND. Field wiring is exposed to a 3.3V logic pin rather than to the protected switched output.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design contains a clear connectivity anomaly: the SPI clock net \"SCK\" is bridged with the USB D+ line (U1 GPIO19/USB_D+, J12 A6/B6) so the shared thermocouple SPI bus is shorted to USB data, contradicting the spec's GPIO5-only SCK. A second, lower-confidence issue is screw terminal J5 being wired to the GPIO1 heater-control net instead of the switched heater output."
}

